# Sign in with Apple

Let your users sign in to Nocturne with their Apple Account. This guide shows how to make a
Services ID and a private key in your Apple Developer account, and how to add them to Nocturne.

Sign in with Apple is not the same as other providers:

- Apple does not use a fixed client secret. Nocturne signs a new one each time from your private key.
- Apple sends the user back with a form POST to its own return URL,
  `/api/auth/oidc/apple/callback`, not to the usual callback URL.
- Apple sends the user's name only one time, on the first sign-in.
- Users can hide their email. Apple then gives a relay address, for example
  `abc123@privaterelay.appleid.com`.

You must have a paid Apple Developer Program membership.

<Callout type="warning" title="No localhost">

  Apple accepts only HTTPS return URLs on a domain name. It does not accept
  <code>localhost</code> or an IP address. To test on your computer, use a tunnel that gives
  you a public HTTPS domain.

</Callout>

## 1. Get your Team ID

1. Go to [developer.apple.com/account](https://developer.apple.com/account).
2. Find the **Team ID** under **Membership details**. It has 10 characters.

## 2. Make an App ID

Apple groups a Services ID under a primary App ID. If you already have an App ID with Sign in
with Apple, use it and go to step 3.

1. Go to **Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles > Identifiers** and click **+**.
2. Select **App IDs**, then **App**.
3. Type a description and a bundle ID, for example `com.example.nocturne`.
4. Under **Capabilities**, select **Sign in with Apple**.
5. Click **Continue**, then **Register**.

## 3. Make a Services ID

The Services ID is the client ID that Nocturne gives to Apple.

1. In **Identifiers**, click **+**, select **Services IDs** and click **Continue**.
2. Type a description and an identifier, for example `com.example.nocturne.signin`.
3. Click **Continue**, then **Register**.
4. Open the new Services ID. Select **Sign in with Apple** and click **Configure**.
5. Set **Primary App ID** to the App ID from step 2.
6. Under **Domains and Subdomains**, add your Nocturne domain, for example `your-instance.com`.
7. Under **Return URLs**, add:

```
https://your-instance.com/api/auth/oidc/apple/callback
```

8. Click **Next**, then **Done**, then **Save**.

<Callout type="tip" title="One URL for all tenants">

  Register only the apex domain. Nocturne sends each sign-in back to the tenant it started on,
  so you do not register a URL for each tenant.

</Callout>

## 4. Make a private key

1. Go to **Keys** and click **+**.
2. Type a key name and select **Sign in with Apple**. Click **Configure** and select the App ID
   from step 2.
3. Click **Continue**, then **Register**.
4. Write down the **Key ID**. It has 10 characters.
5. Click **Download** to get the `.p8` file. You can download it only one time. Keep it safe.

## 5. Add the server-to-server notification endpoint

Apple tells Nocturne when a user stops using Sign in with Apple with your app, or deletes their
Apple Account. Nocturne then ends that user's sessions.

1. Go to **Identifiers** and open the App ID from step 2.
2. Next to **Sign in with Apple**, click **Edit** (or **Configure**).
3. Under **Server-to-Server Notification Endpoint**, type:

```
https://your-instance.com/api/auth/oidc/apple/notifications
```

4. Click **Save**.

Apple can address a notification to the App ID and not to the Services ID. Set **Primary App
ID** in step 6, so that Nocturne accepts both.

## 6. Add the provider in Nocturne

### Option A: the admin UI

<PlatformAdminNote />

1. Sign in to your Nocturne instance as a platform admin.
2. Go to **Settings > Administration** (`/settings/admin`) and open the **Identity Providers** tab.
3. Click **Add Provider** and set **Type** to **Sign in with Apple**. Nocturne fills in the
   issuer, scopes, icon and button color.
4. Fill in:

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Name** | `Apple` |
| **Services ID** | The identifier from step 3 |
| **Team ID** | The Team ID from step 1 |
| **Key ID** | The Key ID from step 4 |
| **Primary App ID** | The bundle ID of the App ID from step 2 |
| **Private key** | All of the `.p8` file from step 4, including the `BEGIN` and `END` lines |

5. Click **Save**.

### Option B: configuration

```jsonc
"Oidc": {
  "Providers": [
    {
      "Name": "Apple",
      "ProviderType": "Apple",
      "IssuerUrl": "https://appleid.apple.com",
      "ClientId": "com.example.nocturne.signin",
      "Apple": {
        "TeamId": "A1B2C3D4E5",
        "KeyId": "F6G7H8I9J0",
        "PrimaryAppId": "com.example.nocturne"
      },
      "Icon": "apple",
      "ButtonColor": "#000000"
    }
  ]
}
```

Put the contents of the `.p8` file in `ClientSecret`. Do not put it in `appsettings.json`:

```bash
# Local development (user-secrets)
dotnet user-secrets set "Oidc:Providers:0:ClientSecret" "$(cat AuthKey_F6G7H8I9J0.p8)"

# Production (environment variable)
Oidc__Providers__0__ClientSecret="$(cat AuthKey_F6G7H8I9J0.p8)"
```

## 7. Test it

1. Open your Nocturne login page in a private window.
2. Click **Sign in with Apple**.
3. Sign in with your Apple Account. Apple asks if it can share your name and email.
4. Apple sends you back to Nocturne, and you are signed in.

## When a user leaves

- **A user removes Apple from their account in Nocturne:** Nocturne tells Apple to revoke its
  grant. The next time the user signs in with Apple, Apple asks again for their name and email.
- **A user's account is deleted:** Nocturne revokes the grant for each linked Apple Account.
- **A user stops using Sign in with Apple with your app** (in their Apple Account settings):
  Apple tells Nocturne, and Nocturne ends the user's sessions. The Apple Account stays linked,
  so the user can sign in with Apple again.
- **A user deletes their Apple Account:** Apple tells Nocturne. Nocturne removes the link to
  that Apple Account and ends the user's sessions.

<Callout type="info" title="Relay email">

  To send email to a relay address, register your sending domain in your Apple Developer
  account under **Services > Sign in with Apple for Email Communication**. If you do not,
  Apple does not deliver the email.

</Callout>
